PLUGIN SCHEDULE COORDINATION
============================

Unattended plugin methods require a confirmed shared Redis slot claim and a
successful read of the operator's current schedule settings. A missing client,
failed claim, lost claim reply, unreadable settings row or absent database session
pauses the attempt. There is no per-worker execution fallback. An unreadable
settings row is different from a successful query finding no row; only the latter
uses the manifest's enabled_by_default value.

The API worker marks its plugin_scheduler subsystem degraded while one of its
active schedules has such a refusal. The authenticated plugin schedules API
exposes blocked_this_worker with a reason and the time the local refusal began.
It is a diagnostic for the worker answering the request, not a global status or
an execution record. Refused attempts do not increment runs_this_worker or
failures_this_worker, and do not overwrite last_run with fictitious work.

Client construction is retried on later scheduler ticks. A confirmed Redis
response clears a coordination refusal; a successful settings read clears a
settings refusal. A job is still skipped when another worker already owns the
slot or the operator has disabled it. Missed historical slots are not replayed.
A request that successfully acquired a slot but could not read settings waits
for a later slot; the claim is not deleted to force a retry.

This does not guarantee exactly-once external effects. Claim eviction, Redis
state loss or restoration of an older Redis snapshot can remove a slot marker.
Plugin implementations must use durable external idempotency for writes and
reconcile uncertain outcomes rather than blindly retrying them. Restoring a
production installation still requires fencing the old writers and reviewing
external effects. The local pause diagnostics do not survive a process restart;
they are re-established when the worker next attempts the schedule.

Existing installations that depended on continuing schedules during a Redis or
database outage now pause them instead. Alert on degraded subsystem health and
inspect the authenticated schedules API. Do not interpret an absent last_run as
proof that a monitoring scan completed or that no drift exists.
